Most ways to scale LLM “autoresearch” agents—run longer, add more agents, let them share ideas—eventually stall as agents get stuck editing the same code over and over.

This new paper shows the fix is simple: forget. Periodically wipe every agent’s memory, keep only the best solution, and start fresh. The result? Up to 15% better performance than parallel teams, and up to 39% better than a lone agent, across program optimization, kernel tuning, and model training.

For open-ended discovery, the key isn’t just more compute—it’s knowing when to let go of the past.

